  anyone who's already relying on it.

  - [1mHard cut at max length[0m — [38;5;153m"The quick brown fox"[39m at 12 → [38;5;153m"The quic..."[39m. Dead simple, one [38;5;153mslice[39m, no edge cases beyond a
    tiny max length. Costs you mid-word garbage on exactly the inputs people see most (truncated text is user-facing by
    definition). Rules nothing out; you could add boundary logic later, but that's the breaking change.
  - [1mWord boundary, hard cut as fallback[0m — same input → [38;5;153m"The quick..."[39m. Reads as intended text rather than a glitch.
    Costs about four extra lines and one real edge case: a single token longer than the budget has no boundary to find,
    so it falls back to the hard cut. Strictly a superset — it [3mis[0m the hard cut whenever no boundary exists.

  [1mI recommend the word boundary.[0m The fallback means you get the simple behavior for free in the cases where the simple
  behavior is all that's available, and the extra complexity is one [38;5;153mlastIndexOf(' ')[39m — not enough savings in option 1 to
  buy a worse default you'd have to break to fix.

  Three details I'd settle the same way under either option:

  1. [1mThe [38;5;153m'...'[39m counts inside the budget[0m, so the result never exceeds [38;5;153mmaxLength[39m. "Cut strings longer than a max length"
     reads as a guarantee, and a function that returns 23 characters when you asked for 20 is a bug waiting to surface
     in a fixed-width context.
  2. [1mTruncate runs after case, before prefix/suffix[0m — so [38;5;153mprefix[39m/[38;5;153msuffix[39m always survive intact. An ellipsis landing after
     your suffix would be nonsense.
  3. [1mAPI shape follows the existing flat pattern[0m: [38;5;153mformat(str, { truncate: 20 })[39m, where the value is the max length.
     Strings at or under the limit are returned untouched.

